(>> 2.4 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: Explain the Dangling-Else problem in C programming and how it is resolved) Tj
T*
(syntactically.) Tj
T*
(Answer: The dangling-else problem arises in nested selection statements when an 'else') Tj
T*
(keyword follows an inner 'if' inside an outer 'if' without enclosing braces: 'if \(cond1\)) Tj
T*
(if \(cond2\) stmt1; else stmt2;'. Semantically, it is ambiguous whether 'else' pairs with) Tj
T*
(outer or inner 'if'. In C, grammar dictates that an 'else' is always associated with the) Tj
T*
(NEAREST PRECEDING UNMATCHED 'if' within the same block scope. Therefore, stmt2 belongs) Tj
T*
(to 'if \(cond2\)'. To pair else with the outer if, compound braces must be used to close) Tj
T*
(inner if explicitly: 'if \(cond1\) { if \(cond2\) stmt1; } else stmt2;'.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Compare and contrast entry-controlled loops \(for, while\) with exit-controlled) Tj
T*
(loops \(do-while\).) Tj
T*
(Answer: \(1\) Execution order: Entry-controlled loops evaluate test conditions BEFORE) Tj
T*
(executing loop body. If condition evaluates to false initially, body is completely) Tj
T*
(bypassed \(minimum execution count = 0\). Exit-controlled loops \(do-while\) execute body) Tj
T*
(FIRST before checking test condition at bottom, guaranteeing body executes AT LEAST) Tj
T*
(ONCE. \(2\) Semicolon syntax: for and while headers do not end with semicolon. do-while) Tj
T*
(requires mandatory terminating semicolon following condition 'do { ... } while \(cond\);'.) Tj
T*
(\(3\) Use case: Entry-controlled loops preferred when bounds are known beforehand;) Tj
T*
(do-while ideal for interactive menu-driven programs where options must be displayed) Tj
T*
(before user choice can be tested.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
